function overviewEnglish(){ return `${nav('overview')}<main class="overview-page"><section class="overview-hero"><div class="crumb"><a href="#home">${language==='zh'?'首页':'Home'}</a><span>/</span><span>Understand CVID</span></div><p class="eyebrow">CVID and antibody deficiency</p><h1>Common Variable<br><em>Immunodeficiency</em></h1><p class="overview-lede">CVID is a diverse group of immune conditions in which the body does not make enough effective antibodies. It can involve much more than infections, and no two people have exactly the same pattern.</p><button class="text-link inline-scroll" type="button" data-scroll-target="cvid-basics">Start with the essential picture ${icon('arrow')}</button><div class="overview-sun" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></section><section class="glance-section" id="cvid-basics"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p><h2>The essential picture.</h2></div><div class="glance-grid four"><button type="button" data-scroll-target="guide-immune"><span>01</span><h3>Antibody failure</h3><p>IgG is persistently low, usually with low IgA and/or IgM, and specific antibody responses are impaired.</p><small>Open explanation →</small></button><button type="button" data-scroll-target="guide-complications"><span>02</span><h3>More than infection</h3><p>Lung, gut, autoimmune, granulomatous and lymphoproliferative complications can be part of CVID.</p><small>Open explanation →</small></button><button type="button" data-scroll-target="guide-diagnosis"><span>03</span><h3>An umbrella diagnosis</h3><p>Some people have a defined monogenic condition; in many others, the molecular cause remains unknown.</p><small>Open explanation →</small></button><button type="button" data-scroll-target="guide-management"><span>04</span><h3>Lifelong follow-up</h3><p>Immunoglobulin replacement reduces infections, while other complications need individual specialist care.</p><small>Open explanation →</small></button></div></section><section class="cvid-guide"><div class="guide-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Understand CVID</p><h2>Four questions patients commonly ask.</h2><p>Select a question to open or close its explanation. Use this as a map for conversations with an immunology team—not as a self-diagnosis checklist.</p></div><div class="guide-sections"><details id="guide-immune" open><summary><span>01</span><h3>What is happening in the immune system?</h3><b aria-hidden="true">+</b></summary><div class="guide-body"><p>B cells are present in many people with CVID but do not mature into effective memory B cells and antibody-producing plasma cells as expected. T-cell help, immune checkpoints and inflammatory pathways may also be involved.</p></div></details><details id="guide-complications"><summary><span>02</span><h3>What symptoms and complications are connected?</h3><b aria-hidden="true">+</b></summary><div class="guide-body"><p>Recurrent sinus, ear and lung infections are common. Some people also develop bronchiectasis or GLILD, chronic diarrhoea or enteropathy, autoimmune cytopenias, enlarged lymph nodes or spleen, granulomas, liver disease, or a higher risk of lymphoma and gastric cancer.</p></div></details><details id="guide-diagnosis"><summary><span>03</span><h3>How is CVID diagnosed?</h3><b aria-hidden="true">+</b></summary><div class="guide-body"><p>Specialists combine repeated immunoglobulin measurements, antibody responses to vaccines or previous infection, clinical history and immune-cell testing. Secondary causes of low immunoglobulins—and other inborn errors of immunity—must be excluded. Genetic testing can be especially useful with early onset, family history or immune dysregulation.</p></div></details><details id="guide-management"><summary><span>04</span><h3>How is it managed?</h3><b aria-hidden="true">+</b></summary><div class="guide-body"><p>Immunoglobulin replacement and infection prevention are central. Monitoring is tailored to the individual and may involve respiratory, gastroenterology, haematology, liver, rheumatology or genetics teams. Non-infectious complications often need separate treatment decisions.</p></div></details></div></section><section class="knowledge-section"><div class="knowledge-title"><p class="eyebrow">A changing picture</p><h2>What we know—and what researchers are still trying to understand.</h2></div><div class="knowledge-grid"><article class="knowledge-card know"><div class="knowledge-icon">${icon('book')}</div><h3>What is established</h3><ul><li>CVID is clinically and biologically heterogeneous.</li><li>Infections usually improve with adequate immunoglobulin replacement.</li><li>Non-infectious complications are major drivers of long-term burden.</li><li>Multidisciplinary follow-up matters because several organs may be involved.</li></ul></article><article class="knowledge-card learning"><div class="knowledge-icon">${icon('flask')}</div><h3>What remains open</h3><ul><li>Why similar antibody findings lead to very different complications.</li><li>How monogenic, polygenic and epigenetic factors interact.</li><li>Which biomarkers can predict GLILD, enteropathy, autoimmunity or malignancy.</li><li>Which mechanism-directed treatments help specific biological subgroups.</li></ul></article></div></section><section class="source-rail"><p class="eyebrow">Reviewed starting points</p><h2>Read the original sources.</h2><div><a href="https://primaryimmune.org/understanding-primary-immunodeficiency/types-of-pi/common-variable-immune-deficiency-cvid" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Immune Deficiency Foundation</strong><span>Patient & Family Handbook chapter ↗</span></a><a href="https://rarediseases.info.nih.gov/diseases/6140/common-variable-immunodeficiency/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>NIH GARD</strong><span>Disease overview and support resources ↗</span></a><a href="https://iuis.org/committees/iei/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>IUIS</strong><span>2024 classification of inborn errors of immunity ↗</span></a></div><p>Educational content last reviewed: 5 September 2026. It does not replace medical advice.</p></section></main>${footer()}`; }
